== The renormalised action in the broken phase <sec:Sren-broken>

This subsection writes the complete one-loop renormalised action of the broken phase in the linear gauge @eq:Lgf at $xi = 1$, with every counterterm explicit. No loop calculation beyond those of @sec:classes is needed. All formulas of the subsection were obtained by expanding the unbroken-phase action with explicit Pauli and hypercharge matrices, and were reproduced by a second, independent expansion (@sec:supporting).

*Coefficients.* As in @sec:Sren1, every renormalisation constant $delta Z_x$, $delta y_k$, $delta lambda$, $delta m^2$, and every quantity built from them below ($delta Z_(Z Z)$, $delta M^2_W$, $delta T$, $c_k$, $delta m_k$, $delta^(f r)_V$), includes the factor $lfe$. A number written without this factor is marked by the prefactor $16 pi^2 ep$. The finite counterterm is $Delta S^((1))_"ST" = lf sum_alpha r_alpha Q_alpha$; the $r_alpha$ and the coefficients built from them do not include $1\/(16 pi^2)$.

=== Statement <sec:bl-statement>

The renormalised action $S_"ren" = S_0 + delta S^((1)) + Delta S^((1))_"ST"$ of @sec:Sren1 is a functional of the Higgs doublet $H$ and of the other fields and sources $Phi'$. The broken phase is the same functional in the variable $phi = H - Hv$, with the constant doublet $Hv$ of @eq:bp-H:
#eqbox[$ S^"broken"_"ren" [phi, Phi'] = S_"ren" [H = Hv + phi, Phi'] . $ <eq:bl-def>]
This is exact, for three reasons.
+ *The shift is a change of variable.* $Hv$ is constant, so $partial_mu H = partial_mu phi$ and $delta \/ delta phi = delta \/ delta H$. The effective action of the broken phase is $Gamma[Hv + phi]$, with $Gamma$ the effective action generated by $S_"ren"$. $Hv$ is not a stationary point of $Gamma$ beyond tree level, so tadpole graphs are part of every Green's function.
+ *The counterterms are local polynomials in $H$.* $Gamma^((1))$ is defined for every background $H$. Its pole part and the breaking $X_1$ are local polynomials in $H$, $H^dagger$ of dimension $<= 4$ and $<= 5$, so they are fixed by their Taylor coefficients at $H = 0$, i.e. by the Green's functions of @sec:classes. $delta S^((1))$ makes $Gamma^((1)) [H]$ finite and $Delta S^((1))_"ST"$ restores $ST(Gamma) = 0$ for every configuration of $H$, in particular for $H = Hv + phi$. Point 2 of @sec:bp-shift spells out why masses and couplings proportional to $v$ change nothing in this argument.
+ *The gauge fixing does not depend on $H$.* @eq:Lgf contains only $b^a$, $A^a$, $agh^a$, $gh^a$. The gauge-fixing, ghost and source terms are therefore the same functionals, the Slavnov--Taylor operator @eq:ST and the linear identities @eq:linear-ids keep their form with $delta \/ delta H = delta \/ delta phi$, and the BRST transformation of $phi$ is
$ sop phi = i wm (Hv + phi), quad quad i wm Hv = vec(i M_W gh^+, -i M_Z gh^Z \/ sqrt(2)) , $ <eq:bl-sphi>
with $wm = Tg^a_H gh^a$ and the ghost fields $gh^plus.minus$, $gh^Z$ of @eq:bl-rot. Rigid $"SU"(2)_L times "U"(1)_Y$ invariance stays exact, realised non-linearly on $phi$.

Additional input would be needed only if the gauge fixing were changed to one that depends on $H$ and $v$, such as the $R_xi$ gauge @eq:bp-Rxi: that is a different action, whose new vertices give Green's functions not computed here (@sec:bp-gauge). Nothing below uses it.

=== Parameters and fields <sec:bl-fields>

*Vacuum value.* $v$ is defined by the minimum of the tree-level potential written with the $overline("MS")$ renormalised parameters,
$ m^2 = - lambda v^2, quad m^2 < 0 . $ <eq:bl-vev>
This is a convention: $v$ is not a parameter of $S_"ren"$ but a function of $m^2$ and $lambda$. With it the tadpole graphs are kept, and $delta S^((1))$ contains a tadpole counterterm (@eq:bl-dS-scalar). Other tadpole conventions, e.g. $v$ at the minimum of the effective potential, differ by a finite reparametrisation of $v$ (@sec:bp-tadpole).

*Scalars.* The doublet is expanded as
$ H = Hv + phi, quad Hv = vec(0, v\/sqrt(2)), quad phi = vec(G^+, (h + i G^0)\/sqrt(2)) , $ <eq:bp-H>
with $h$, $G^0$ real and $G^- = (G^+)^*$.

*Gauge fields.* The weak mixing angle and the unit of electric charge are
$ c_W = gw/sqrt(gw^2 + gy^2), quad s_W = gy/sqrt(gw^2 + gy^2), quad e = gw s_W = gy c_W . $ <eq:bp-rot>
The rotation is applied with the same angle to the gauge fields, ghosts, antighosts, $b^a$ and the sources $srcA^mu_a$, $srcC_a$:
$ X^plus.minus = (X^1 minus.plus i X^2)/sqrt(2), quad X^Z = c_W X^3 - s_W X^B, quad X^gamma = s_W X^3 + c_W X^B \
  "for" quad X in {A_mu, gh, agh, b, srcA^mu, srcC} , $ <eq:bl-rot>
with $X^B$ the hypercharge component ($A^B_mu = B_mu$, $A^gamma_mu = Ag_mu$, $A^Z_mu = Z_mu$, $A^plus.minus_mu = W^plus.minus_mu$). The photon is written $Ag_mu$ because $A^a_mu$ denotes the twelve gauge fields. For two adjoint vectors $sum_(a in "SU"(2) times "U"(1)) X^a Y^a = X^+ Y^- + X^- Y^+ + X^Z Y^Z + X^gamma Y^gamma$. Two combinations recur:
$ gw W^3_mu = e Ag_mu + gw c_W Z_mu, quad quad gy B_mu = e Ag_mu - gy s_W Z_mu . $ <eq:bl-combo>
Since $Hv$ is constant, the gauge-boson mass terms come from
$ (D_mu Hv)^dagger D^mu Hv = 1/2 cal(M)^2_(a b) A^a dot A^b, quad cal(M)^2_(a b) = Hv^dagger {Tg^a_H, Tg^b_H} Hv , $ <eq:bp-masses>
with $a, b$ over the four $"SU"(2)_L times "U"(1)_Y$ components and ${X, Y} = X Y + Y X$. The eigenvalues of $cal(M)^2$ are $M_W^2$ (twice), $M_Z^2$ and $0$ (photon). The tree-level masses are
$ M_W = (gw v)/2, quad M_Z = M_W / c_W, quad M_h^2 = 2 lambda v^2, quad m_k = (y_k v)/sqrt(2) quad (k = u, d, e), \
  M_gamma = M_g = M_(G^plus.minus) = M_(G^0) = m_nu = 0 . $ <eq:bl-masses>
The Goldstone fields are massless in this gauge, and all ghosts are massless.

*Fermions.* The mass eigenstates are Dirac fields built from the physical components, with sterile partners built from the $chi_f$:
$ & u = PL Psi^1_Q + PR Psi_U, quad d = PL Psi^2_Q + PR Psi_D, quad e = PL Psi^2_L + PR Psi_E, quad nu = Psi^1_L , \
  & u' = PR Psi^1_Q + PL Psi_U, quad d' = PR Psi^2_Q + PL Psi_D, quad e' = PR Psi^2_L + PL Psi_E . $ <eq:bl-fermions>
$Psi^1$, $Psi^2$ are the upper and lower $"SU"(2)_L$ components of a doublet. $f_upright(L) = PL f$ and $f_upright(R) = PR f$ ($f = u, d, e$) and $nu_upright(L) = PL nu$ are physical; $PR nu$ is the sterile partner of $nu_upright(L)$. Quarks carry a colour index. The electric charge is $q_f = T^3_f + Y_f$ ($q_u = 2/3$, $q_d = -1/3$, $q_e = -1$, $q_nu = 0$), with $T^3_f$ the isospin of $f_upright(L)$; on a multiplet $q = T^3 + Y$ is the charge matrix. $N^c_f$ is the colour multiplicity ($3$ for quarks, $1$ for leptons). Bilinears such as $overline(f) f = overline(f)_upright(L) f_upright(R) + overline(f)_upright(R) f_upright(L)$ and $overline(f) g5 f = overline(f) PR f - overline(f) PL f$ contain physical components only.

=== The tree action <sec:bl-tree>

*Quadratic part.* All indices are $d$-dimensional, $X_(mu nu) = partial_mu X_nu - partial_nu X_mu$, and $b^a$ has been eliminated ($b^a = -partial dot A^a$ at $xi = 1$; before elimination the gauge fixing is $b^+ partial dot W^- + b^- partial dot W^+ + b^Z partial dot Z + b^gamma partial dot Ag + b^A partial dot G^A + b^+ b^- + 1/2 (b^Z b^Z + b^gamma b^gamma + b^A b^A)$).
$ cal(L)^V_2 = & - 1/2 W^+_(mu nu) W^(- mu nu) - (partial dot W^+)(partial dot W^-) + M_W^2 W^+ dot W^-
  - 1/4 Z_(mu nu) Z^(mu nu) - 1/2 (partial dot Z)^2 + 1/2 M_Z^2 Z dot Z \
  & - 1/4 Ag_(mu nu) A^(gamma mu nu) - 1/2 (partial dot Ag)^2 - 1/4 G^A_(mu nu) G^(A mu nu) - 1/2 (partial dot G^A)^2 , $ <eq:bl-L2V>
$ cal(L)^(V G)_2 = M_Z Z^mu partial_mu G^0 + i M_W (W^-_mu partial^mu G^+ - W^+_mu partial^mu G^-) , $ <eq:bl-L2mix>
$ cal(L)^S_2 = partial_mu G^+ partial^mu G^- + 1/2 (partial G^0)^2 + 1/2 (partial h)^2 - 1/2 M_h^2 h^2 - v (m^2 + lambda v^2) h , $ <eq:bl-L2S>
$ cal(L)^F_2 = sum_(f = u, d, e) [ overline(f) i slb(partial) f + overline(f)' i slb(partial) f' + overline(f) i slh(partial) f' + overline(f)' i slh(partial) f - m_f overline(f) f ] + overline(nu) i sl(partial) nu , $ <eq:bl-L2F>
$ cal(L)^(gh)_2 = partial^mu agh^+ partial_mu gh^- + partial^mu agh^- partial_mu gh^+ + partial^mu agh^Z partial_mu gh^Z + partial^mu agh^gamma partial_mu gh^gamma + partial^mu agh^A partial_mu gh^A . $ <eq:bl-L2gh>
The mixing @eq:bl-L2mix remains because the gauge fixing is linear: only an $H$-dependent gauge fixing such as $R_xi$ cancels it (@eq:bp-tree). The tadpole term of @eq:bl-L2S vanishes by @eq:bl-vev. Ghosts are massless and couple only to gauge fields: there is no ghost--scalar vertex in this gauge. The source term $srcH^dagger sop H + "h.c."$ contains with @eq:bl-sphi the bilinear $srcH^dagger (i M_W gh^+, -i M_Z gh^Z\/sqrt(2))^T + "h.c."$, a two-point vertex between a source and a ghost (no propagator).

*Boson propagators.* For $Phi = (Z_mu, G^0)$ write $S_2 = 1/2 integral_k Phi(-k)^T Mk (k) Phi(k)$ with $Phi(k)$ the coefficient of $e^(-i k dot x)$; for $Phi = (W^+_mu, G^+)$ write $S_2 = integral_k Phi_- (-k)^T Mk (k) Phi(k)$ with $Phi_- = (W^-_mu, G^-)$. From @eq:bl-L2V and @eq:bl-L2mix the kinetic matrices are
$ Mk^(Z G^0) (k) = mat(delim: "[", (M_Z^2 - k^2) g^(mu nu), -i M_Z k^mu; i M_Z k^nu, k^2), quad quad
  Mk^(W G) (k) = mat(delim: "[", (M_W^2 - k^2) g^(mu nu), M_W k^mu; M_W k^nu, k^2) . $ <eq:bl-Mkin>
The propagator is $i Mk (k)^(-1)$, with momentum $k$ flowing from the second field to the first:
#eqbox[$ & ⟨Z_mu Z_nu⟩ = (-i)/(k^2 - M_Z^2) (g_(mu nu) - (k_mu k_nu)/k^2) - (i k_mu k_nu)/(k^2)^2, quad
  ⟨W^+_mu W^-_nu⟩ = (-i)/(k^2 - M_W^2) (g_(mu nu) - (k_mu k_nu)/k^2) - (i k_mu k_nu)/(k^2)^2, \
  & ⟨Z_mu G^0⟩ = - ⟨G^0 Z_mu⟩ = (M_Z k_mu)/(k^2)^2, quad ⟨W^+_mu G^-⟩ = ⟨G^+ W^-_mu⟩ = (i M_W k_mu)/(k^2)^2, \
  & ⟨G^0 G^0⟩ = i/k^2 - (i M_Z^2)/(k^2)^2, quad ⟨G^+ G^-⟩ = i/k^2 - (i M_W^2)/(k^2)^2 . $ <eq:bl-prop>]
The photon and the gluon have $-i g_(mu nu) \/ k^2$, $h$ has $i\/(k^2 - M_h^2)$, all ghosts $i\/k^2$. The longitudinal and Goldstone parts have double poles at $k^2 = 0$: the unphysical sector of the linear gauge.
#check[The kinetic matrices @eq:bl-Mkin were built from @eq:bl-L2V and @eq:bl-L2mix in explicit components; $i Mk^(-1)$ agrees with @eq:bl-prop at random $k$ and masses, exactly in rational arithmetic, in $d = 4$ and $d = 6$. The mixing @eq:bl-L2mix and the masses were re-derived from @eq:LHiggs with $H = Hv + phi$ and explicit Pauli matrices.]

*Fermion propagator.* The mass term of $u$ couples only $u_upright(L) = PL Psi^1_Q$ and $u_upright(R) = PR Psi_U$. For the pair $Phi = (u, u')$ of Dirac fields the quadratic Lagrangian @eq:bl-L2F is $overline(Phi) Mk (i partial) Phi$, and in momentum space ($i partial -> p$, $p$ the momentum along the fermion line) the block $(i, j)$ of $i Mk^(-1)$ is $⟨Phi_i overline(Phi)_j⟩$:
#eqbox[$ & Mk (p) = mat(delim: "[", slb(p) - m, slh(p); slh(p), slb(p)), \
  & i Mk^(-1)(p) = i/((p^2)^2 - m^2 bar(p)^2) mat(delim: "[", p^2 slb(p) + m bar(p)^2, p^2 slh(p) + m slb(p) slh(p); p^2 slh(p) - m slb(p) slh(p), (p^2 - m^2) slb(p) + m hat(p)^2) , $ <eq:bp-prop>]
with $m = m_u$; the same holds for $(d, d')$ and $(e, e')$. The neutrino has the massless propagator of @eq:propagators. The physical block $(Mk^(-1))_(11)$ is the propagator of $u$; it is not the standard massive propagator (@sec:bp-fermion).
#check[$Mk$ follows from the $(Psi^1_Q, Psi_U)$ Lagrangian @eq:Lferm-weyl by the change of basis @eq:bl-fermions. $Mk$ and all four blocks of $Mk^(-1)$ agree with explicit BMHV matrices in $d = 6, 8$ at random momenta and masses. $m = 0$ gives @eq:propagators. $hat(p) = 0$ gives $i (slb(p) + m)\/(bar(p)^2 - m^2)$ in the physical block and $i slb(p)\/bar(p)^2$ in the sterile block.]

*Interaction part.* With $H = Hv + phi$,
$ cal(L)_"int" = & [(D_mu H)^dagger D^mu H]_"int" - lambda v h Phi_2 - lambda/4 Phi_2^2 + cal(L)_"Yuk" \
  & - 1/4 [F^a_(mu nu) F^(a mu nu)]_"int" + sum_f overline(Psi)_f gbar^mu Am^f_mu P_(c_f) Psi_f + partial^mu agh^a fg^(a b c) A^b_mu gh^c , $ <eq:bl-Lint>
$Phi_2 = h^2 + (G^0)^2 + 2 G^+ G^-$, where "int" removes the quadratic terms above. In the mass basis the matrix gauge field of @eq:matrix-fields is, on any multiplet,
$ Am^((W))_mu + Am^((B))_mu = gw/sqrt(2) (tau^+ W^+_mu + tau^- W^-_mu) + e q Ag_mu + gw/c_W (T^3 - s_W^2 q) Z_mu , $ <eq:bl-Am>
with $tau^plus.minus = (tau^1 plus.minus i tau^2)\/2$ and $T^3 = tau^3\/2$ on doublets and $q = T^3 + Y$. The scalar--gauge terms are those of $(D_mu (Hv + phi))^dagger D^mu (Hv + phi)$; the ones linear in $phi$ are $(2 h\/v)(M_W^2 W^+ dot W^- + 1/2 M_Z^2 Z dot Z) + M_W (e Ag - gy s_W Z) dot (G^+ W^- + G^- W^+)$. The fermion currents are $e q_f Ag dot overline(f) gbar f + (gw\/c_W) Z dot overline(f) gbar (T^3_f PL - s_W^2 q_f) f + gw\/sqrt(2) thin (W^+ dot overline(u) gbar PL d + W^+ dot overline(nu) gbar PL e + "h.c.") + gs G^A dot (overline(u) gbar T^A u + overline(d) gbar T^A d)$. The Yukawa term @eq:LYuk is
$ cal(L)_"Yuk" = & - sum_(f = u, d, e) m_f (1 + h/v) overline(f) f + i (m_u)/v G^0 overline(u) g5 u - i (m_d)/v G^0 overline(d) g5 d - i (m_e)/v G^0 overline(e) g5 e \
  & + (sqrt(2) m_u)/v (G^+ overline(u) PL d + G^- overline(d) PR u) - (sqrt(2) m_d)/v (G^+ overline(u) PR d + G^- overline(d) PL u) - (sqrt(2) m_e)/v (G^+ overline(nu) PR e + G^- overline(e) PL nu) . $ <eq:bl-yuk>
#check[@eq:bl-yuk was obtained from @eq:LYuk with $tilde(H)_i = epsilon_(i j) H^*_j$ and the component map @eq:bl-fermions, bilinear by bilinear; @eq:bl-Am and the potential of @eq:bl-Lint (with @eq:bl-vev) by explicit expansion.]

=== The divergent counterterm <sec:bl-dS>

The group invariants and the combinations $C_H$, $Yt$, $Y_4$, $G_4$, $cal(Y)_f$, $nu_(f r)$ are those defined at the start of @sec:Sren1. With the tree masses @eq:bl-masses, $C_H$, $Yt$, $Y_4$ and $G_4$ are
$ C_H = (2 M_W^2 + M_Z^2)/v^2, quad Yt = (2 (3 m_u^2 + 3 m_d^2 + m_e^2))/v^2, \ Y_4 = (4 (3 m_u^4 + 3 m_d^4 + m_e^4))/v^4, quad G_4 = (16 (2 M_W^4 + M_Z^4))/v^4 . $ <eq:bl-sums>

*(a) Invariant part.* $delta S^((1))_"inv"$ is the first-order term of $S_0$ written in the bare quantities @eq:c-bare (@tab:bl-Z). In the broken phase the same substitution acts on $H = Hv + phi$: $H_0 = Z_H^(1\/2) (Hv + phi)$. $v$ is not renormalised (it is a function of the renormalised $m^2$, $lambda$), and the rotation @eq:bl-rot uses the renormalised $gw$, $gy$. The gauge fixing is not renormalised. The values of all constants are those of @tab:c-Z. @tab:bl-Z gives the short names used below and the two ghost-sector combinations $tilde(Z)_3 - 1$ and $tilde(Z)_1 - 1$.

